TI  - Prior heavy-intensity exercise's enhancement of oxygen-uptake kinetics and 
      short-term high-intensity exercise performance independent of aerobic-training 
      status.
PG  - 339-45
LID - 10.1123/ijspp.2014-0131 [doi]
AB  - Prior high-intensity exercise can improve exercise performance during 
      severe-intensity exercise. These positive alterations have been attributed, at 
      least in part, to enhancement of overall oxygen-uptake (VO2) kinetics. PURPOSE: 
      To determine the effects of prior heavy-intensity exercise on VO2 kinetics and 
      short-term high-intensity exercise performance in individuals with different 
      aerobic-training statuses. METHODS: Fifteen active subjects (UT; VO2max = 43.8 ± 
      6.3 mL · kg-1 · min-1) and 10 well-trained endurance cyclists (T; VO2max = 66.7 ± 
      6.7 mL · kg-1 · min-1) performed the following protocols: an incremental test to 
      determine lactate threshold and VO2max, 4 maximal constant-load tests to estimate 
      critical power, and two 3-min bouts of cycle exercise, involving 2 min of 
      constant-work-rate exercise at severe intensity followed by a 1-min all-out 
      sprint test. This trial was performed without prior intervention and 10 min after 
      prior heavy-intensity exercise (ie, 6 min at 90% critical power). RESULTS: The 
      mean response time of VO2 was shortened after prior exercise for both UT (30.7 ± 
      9.2 vs 24.1 ± 7.2 s) and T (31.8 ± 5.2 vs 25.4 ± 4.3 s), but no 
      group-by-condition interaction was detected. The end-sprint performance (ie, mean 
      power output) was improved in both groups (UT ~4.7%, T ~2.0%; P < .05) by prior 
      exercise. CONCLUSION: The effect of prior heavy-intensity exercise on overall VO2 
      kinetics and short-term high-intensity exercise performance is independent of 
      aerobic-training status.
