DP  - 1991 Sep
TI  - Psychobiologic effects of 3 d of increased training in female and male swimmers.
PG  - 1055-61
AB  - Eighteen female and 22 male college swimmers completed a paced 182.9 m swim 
      before and after a 72 h period of increased training. Training volume was 
      increased from 6,800 to 11,200 m.d-1 for the females and from 8,800 to 12,950 
      m.d-1 for the males. Salivary cortisol, heart rate, stroke mechanics, as well as 
      overall and local ratings of perceived exertion (RPE) were measured in 
      conjunction with the two swim tests. Mood states, as measured by the Profile of 
      Mood States, and ratings of perceived muscle soreness were assessed daily. 
      Significant (P less than 0.005) elevations in stroke frequency, overall and local 
      RPE, fatigue, overall mood, and muscle soreness levels occurred in association 
      with the increased training. Significant (P less than 0.005) reductions in vigor 
      and stroke length were also observed as a consequence of the greater training 
      load. The main effects for gender and the gender by trial interactions were not 
      significant for each variable tested. It was concluded that: 1) affective, 
      biomechanical, and perceptual variables are sensitive to a 3 d increase in the 
      volume of swim training, 2) heart rate and cortisol levels are not influenced by 
      the type of increased training employed in this study, and 3) female and male 
      college swimmers do not differ in their psychological or physiological responses 
      to a rapid increase in training volume.
