TI  - Session-to-session variations of internal load during different small-sided 
      games: a study in professional soccer players.
PG  - 462-474
LID - 10.1080/15438627.2021.1888103 [doi]
AB  - The aim of this study was to analyse variations of internal load across 
      small-sided games (SSG) in professional soccer. Twenty players (mean ± SD; age 
      28.1 ± 4.6 yo, height 176.7 ± 4.9 cm, weight 72.0 ± 7.8 kg) performed 3v3, 4v4, 
      and 6v6 formats with/without goalkeeper and touch limitations. Each condition was 
      repeated over three sessions and heart rate (HR) measures including average HR 
      (HR(avg)), Edwards' training impulse (Edwards' TRIMP) and time in red zone (>80% 
      of maximal HR) were recorded. All measures had trivial-to-moderate typical error 
      (TE) and trivial differences were observed within intervals. The HR(avg) showed 
      less coefficient of variations (0.9% to 1.7%) compared to Edwards'TRIMP(.)min(-1) 
      and red zone(.)min(-1) (2% to 9.7%). A reduction trend in TE was observed when 
      touching limitations or using goalkeepers. Practitioners can use different SSG 
      formats but if the aim is to have less noise at higher intensities, more 
      controlled drills are recommended.
