DP  - 2026 Jun 4
TI  - Perceived exertion reflects exercise-induced pain rather than psychological 
      characteristics in recreational endurance athletes.
LID - 10.1038/s41598-026-55769-2 [doi]
LID - 25605
AB  - In endurance exercise, psychological characteristics such as anxiety, depression, 
      and personality traits may influence ratings of perceived exertion (RPE) and 
      exercise-induced pain (EIP). However, it remains unclear whether these 
      characteristics are independently associated with session RPE (sRPE) or influence 
      the relationship between sRPE and EIP. This study examined the association 
      between sRPE and EIP in recreational endurance athletes and explored the 
      potential role of psychological characteristics in this relationship. A total of 
      144 recreational endurance athletes (41 trail runners, 73 hikers) were enrolled 
      at organized outdoor endurance events conducted on non-technical terrain (≤ T2, 
      Swiss Alpine Club classification) at altitudes below 2500 m during the summer 
      season. Participants rated maximum pain intensity, average pain intensity, and 
      pain unpleasantness, which were averaged to derive a composite EIP score. 
      Psychological assessments included the personality traits, pain catastrophizing, 
      anxiety sensitivity, and symptoms of anxiety and depression. External exercise 
      load was quantified using effort-adjusted pace and exercise duration. EIP was the 
      only variable consistently associated with sRPE, with each one-unit increase 
      corresponding to an approximately 13-19% increase in perceived exertion. This 
      association remained significant after adjustment for all covariates, suggesting 
      that sRPE may primarily reflect concurrent EIP rather than external load or 
      psychological characteristics.
