DP  - 2026 Oct 5
TI  - Noninvasive assessment of the anaerobic threshold in endurance athletes: an 
      evidence-based narrative review of methods, validity, and field feasibility.
LID - 10.1093/bmb/ldag037 [doi]
LID - ldag037
AB  - INTRODUCTION OR BACKGROUND: The anaerobic threshold (AnT) marks the transition 
      between heavy and severe exercise intensity domains and guides training 
      prescription and performance monitoring. Criterion methods (blood lactate and gas 
      exchange) are accurate but invasive, costly, or impractical outside laboratories, 
      prompting the search for noninvasive surrogates. This review evaluates the 
      validity of these estimation procedures rather than the underlying physiology. 
      SOURCES OF DATA: MEDLINE (via PubMed) was searched from inception without 
      language restrictions, supplemented by reference-list and citation searching. 
      Eligible studies compared noninvasive AnT indices, including heart rate 
      variability (HRV; including detrended fluctuation analysis [DFA] α1), cardiac 
      repolarization instability, heart rate deflection/field slope-variation methods, 
      critical power (CP)/ critical speed (CS), near-infrared spectroscopy (NIRS), the 
      talk test, and machine-learning (ML) models, with criterion standards. Risk of 
      bias was assessed using QUADAS-2. Eighty-eight reports were synthesized 
      narratively; no meta-analysis was performed. AREAS OF AGREEMENT: DFA α1, NIRS 
      breakpoints, and CP/CS show strong associations with laboratory criteria and 
      increasing field applicability through wearables. Accuracy and feasibility 
      trade-offs require method selection according to the threshold, sport, and 
      required precision. AREAS OF CONTROVERSY: Correlation indicates group-level 
      association rather than individual agreement; Bland-Altman and reliability 
      analyses are more informative for substitution. Criterion standards are not 
      interchangeable, and heart rate deflection is not consistently identifiable. 
      Evidence in female athletes remains limited. GROWING POINTS: Multimodal wearables 
      integrating HRV, muscle oxygenation, and running power with ML may improve 
      field-based threshold estimation. AREAS FOR DEVELOPING RESEARCH: Priorities 
      include standardized reporting, transparent bias assessment, female-athlete 
      validation, and broader systematic and meta-analytic synthesis.
