TI  - Electrocardiogram during deep breath-hold dives by elite divers.
PG  - 81-91
AB  - A portable ECG recorder was used during breath-hold dives at sea by 3 elite 
      divers to 65 and 45 m. ECG was also recorded during nonimmersed maximal breath 
      holds in the divers and 8 control subjects. Heart rate in the dives decreased 
      rapidly to 20-24 beats.min(-1). During the surface experiments in the divers, 
      bradycardia was much slower in onset, reaching 28-36 beats.min(-1) at the end of 
      the breath holds. The divers showed a more consistent bradycardial response than 
      the controls. The difference in temporal pattern of bradycardia, in the dives and 
      in the breath holds by the divers, may have been due to face immersion in cold 
      water, chest compression, and/or redistribution of blood into the chest with 
      concomitant stimulation of cardiac and other mechanoreceptors. Arrhythmias, 
      mostly supraventricular and ventricular premature complexes, were observed 
      coincidently with the lowest heart rates, presumably reflecting a high vagal 
      tone. In addition, cardiac distention at depth might have made the heart more 
      prone to arrhythmias, while in the surface breath holds hypoxia might have 
      accounted for a similar effect.
