TI  - Consumer-priced wearable sensors combined with deep learning can be used to 
      accurately predict ground reaction forces during various treadmill running 
      conditions.
PG  - e17896
LID - 10.7717/peerj.17896 [doi]
LID - e17896
AB  - Ground reaction force (GRF) data is often collected for the biomechanical 
      analysis of running, due to the performance and injury risk insights that GRF 
      analysis can provide. Traditional methods typically limit GRF collection to 
      controlled lab environments, recent studies have looked to combine the ease of 
      use of wearable sensors with the statistical power of machine learning to 
      estimate continuous GRF data outside of these restrictions. Before such systems 
      can be deployed with confidence outside of the lab they must be shown to be a 
      valid and accurate tool for a wide range of users. The aim of this study was to 
      evaluate how accurately a consumer-priced sensor system could estimate GRFs 
      whilst a heterogeneous group of runners completed a treadmill protocol with three 
      different personalised running speeds and three gradients. Fifty runners (25 
      female, 25 male) wearing pressure insoles made up of 16 resistive sensors and an 
      inertial measurement unit ran at various speeds and gradients on an instrumented 
      treadmill. A long short term memory (LSTM) neural network was trained to estimate 
      both vertical (GRFv) and anteroposterior (GRFap) force traces using leave one 
      subject out validation. The average relative root mean squared error (rRMSE) was 
      3.2% and 3.1%, respectively. The mean (GRFv) rRMSE across the evaluated 
      participants ranged from 0.8% to 8.8% and from 1.3% to 17.3% in the (GRFap) 
      estimation. The findings from this study suggest that current consumer-priced 
      sensors could be used to accurately estimate two-dimensional GRFs for a wide 
      range of runners at a variety of running intensities. The estimated kinetics 
      could be used to provide runners with individualised feedback as well as form the 
      basis of data collection for running injury risk factor studies on a much larger 
      scale than is currently possible with lab based methods.
