      Load.
LID - 10.3390/s21103398 [doi]
LID - 3398
AB  - While the Player Load is a widely-used parameter for physical demand 
      quantification using wearable accelerometers, its calculation is subjected to 
      potential errors related to rotational changes of the reference frame. The aims 
      of this study were (i) to assess the concurrent validity of accelerometry-based 
      Player Load against force plates; (ii) to validate a novel metric, the Accel'Rate 
      overcoming this theoretical issue. Twenty-one recreational athlete males 
      instrumented with two triaxial accelerometers positioned at the upper and lower 
      back performed running-based locomotor movements at low and high intensity over 
      six in-series force plates. We examined the validity of the Player Load and the 
      Accel'Rate by using force plates. Standard error of the estimate was small to 
      moderate for all tested conditions (Player Load: 0.45 to 0.87; Accel'Rate: 0.25 
      to 0.95). Accel'Rate displayed trivial to small mean biases (-1.0 to 6.1 a.u.) 
      while the Player Load displayed systematic very large to extremely large mean 
      biases (17.1 to 226.0 a.u.). These findings demonstrate a better concurrent 
      validity of the Accel'Rate compared to the Player Load. This metric could be used 
      to improve the estimation of whole-body mechanical load, easily accessible in 
      sport training and competition settings.
