IS  - 0195-9131 (Linking)
VI  - 32
IP  - 9
DP  - 2000 Sep
TI  - Cardiac autonomic imbalance in an overtrained athlete.
PG  - 1531-3
AB  - PURPOSE: In order to investigate overtraining-related adaptations in the 
      autonomic nervous system, cardiac autonomic activity was examined in a junior 
      cross-country skier who presented with reduced performance in competitions, early 
      breathlessness during training sessions, and accumulated central fatigue. 
      METHODS: Power spectral analysis of heart rate variability (HRV) was performed 
      before, when overtrained (OT), and after recovery (Rec). RESULTS: In the 
      overtrained state, high frequency (HF) and total powers in the lying position 
      were higher compared with before and after. In normalized units, the increased HF 
      in OT was even more prominent and clearly higher than in any control subject, and 
      it was reversed in Rec. Resting heart rate was slightly reduced in OT and 
      returned to baseline in Rec. CONCLUSIONS: The shift toward increased heart rate 
      variability, particularly in the HF range, together with a reduced resting heart 
      rate suggest a cardiac autonomic imbalance with extensive parasympathetic 
      modulation in this athlete when overtrained.
