IP  - 1
DP  - 2026 Jan 5
TI  - Longitudinal assessment of total daily energy expenditure in professional 
      cyclists supports a maximal sustainable metabolic ceiling.
PG  - 151-160.e2
LID - S0960-9822(25)01545-3 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.cub.2025.11.038 [doi]
AB  - This study investigated whether elite athletes can surpass the proposed ceiling 
      of human energy expenditure, estimated at 2.5 times basal metabolic rate (BMR), 
      over extended periods. Previous research has suggested that this limit represents 
      the maximal sustainable daily energy expenditure, but longitudinal evidence in 
      high-performance populations is scarce. To address this, we estimated total daily 
      energy expenditure (TDEE) in eight male UCI-registered professional cyclists 
      across a full year. TDEE was expressed as a multiple of BMR, termed physical 
      activity level (PAL). Cycling energy expenditure was estimated from power data 
      using validated equations for professional cyclists, while non-exercise energy 
      expenditure and BMR were estimated using methods specific to this population. The 
      results showed an average yearly PAL of 2.52 ± 0.11, which was not significantly 
      different from the proposed 2.5× BMR ceiling. However, when analyses focused only 
      on cycling training days, PAL rose significantly above the limit, averaging 2.70 
      ± 0.14 (p < 0.001). These findings suggest that while professional cyclists can 
      temporarily exceed the threshold during intense activity, the overall long-term 
      average remains constrained near 2.5× BMR. We conclude that even world-class 
      athletes who regularly consume nutritional supplements conform to the theorized 
      limit of sustainable energy expenditure over prolonged periods. Importantly, the 
      elevated expenditure observed during training days highlights the importance of 
      rest days in restoring energy balance and enabling athletes to maintain 
      performance. Thus, overall, the findings reinforce the concept of a maximal 
      sustainable energy ceiling while underscoring the necessity of recovery days for 
      sustainable long-term endurance training.
