TI  - Comparing the effects of small-sided games, skill development, and plyometric 
      training on basketball learning and athletic performance in youth male athletes: 
      A quasi-experimental study.
PG  - 1708-1721
LID - 10.1080/02640414.2026.2692305 [doi]
AB  - This study compared three training approaches on basketball learning and 
      performance in youth male players. Forty-two U14 athletes competing in the 
      Lithuanian Youth Basketball League completed an 8-week in-season 
      quasi-experimental intervention. Participants were assigned to small-sided games 
      (SSG; n = 11), skill-development training (SKILL; n = 11), plyometric training 
      (PLYO; n = 10), or control (n = 10), with two weekly sessions integrated into 
      regular practice. Pre - post assessments included 20-m sprint, countermovement 
      jump (CMJ), change-of-direction speed (CODS), VO(2) max (30-15 IFT), 5 × 5 
      simulated match external load, and basketball learning indices (BALPAI). Linear 
      mixed-effects models examined TIME × GROUP interactions. SKILL improved sprint, 
      aerobic capacity, and technical execution versus control. PLYO enhanced CMJ and 
      final efficacy but reduced match running output (distance/min). SSG showed no 
      clear benefits and was associated with declines in high-intensity actions and 
      decision-making. CODS improved across groups. Adaptations were modality-specific, 
      underscoring the importance of aligning training content with targeted 
      developmental outcomes in youth basketball.
