TI  - Ventilatory thresholds are detectable using DFA-α1 at group level but not 
      individual level during swimming, cycling and running.
LID - 2060.2026.00932 [pii]
LID - 10.1556/2060.2026.00932 [doi]
AB  - BACKGROUND: Ventilatory thresholds (VT1 and VT2) determined from gas-exchange 
      criteria during cardiopulmonary exercise testing (CPET) provide reference points 
      for exercise-intensity prescription, but their routine use is limited in applied 
      settings. Detrended fluctuation analysis alpha 1 (DFA-α1) has emerged as a 
      potential non-invasive marker of physiological transitions during exercise. 
      OBJECTIVE: To quantify and compare DFA-α1 values against CPET-derived ventilatory 
      thresholds and between exercise modes during constant-load swimming, cycling, and 
      running in trained triathletes. METHODS: Seventeen triathletes (13 men, 4 women) 
      completed maximal incremental CPETs in swimming, cycling, and running, after 
      which constant-load trials were undertaken at VT1 and VT2, as determined from 
      CPET using standard ventilatory criteria. R-R intervals were recorded with a 
      Polar V800 and Polar H10 and processed in Kubios HRV Scientific v4.2.0. DFA-α1 
      was calculated using a 120-s moving window with 5-s updates. Between-modality 
      comparisons were performed using repeated-measures ANOVA. RESULTS: At VT1, V˙ O2, 
      heart rate, and PetCO2 differed by modality, whereas DFA-α1 did not differ 
      significantly between swimming, cycling, and running (0.74 ± 0.30, 0.75 ± 0.21, 
      and 0.65 ± 0.20, respectively; P = 0.456). At VT2, V˙ O2 and heart rate again 
      differed by modality, whereas DFA-α1 showed no significant differences between 
      modalities but wide between participant variability (0.42 ± 0.13, 0.37 ± 0.11, 
      and 0.47 ± 0.13, respectively; P = 0.061). CONCLUSION: At the group level, mean 
      DFA-α1 values were numerically close to previously proposed values for VT1 
      (∼0.75) and VT2 (∼0.50). However, the marked inter-individual dispersion across 
      modalities limits the use of fixed DFA-α1 values for individual identification of 
      VT1 and VT2. These findings indicate descriptive group-level correspondence 
      rather than individual-level validity or agreement.
