DP  - 2026 Oct 20
TI  - Allometric Scaling of Left Heart Structure and Function in Elite Cyclists: 
      Development of Sport-Specific Prediction Equations.
PG  - e050142
LID - 10.1161/JAHA.126.050142 [doi]
AB  - BACKGROUND: Elite athletes frequently exceed conventional left ventricular (LV) 
      and left atrial thresholds when guideline-recommended linear scaling for body 
      size is applied, prompting unnecessary investigation. Few athlete-specific 
      allometric equations yield percentage predicted values or Z scores. We aimed to 
      develop allometric equations for LV and left atrial structure and function in 
      elite cyclists, accounting for body size, sex, discipline, and heart rate, and to 
      compare these with body surface area (BSA) indexing. METHODS: We evaluated 181 
      elite cyclists (105 male, 76 female; age 22.7±5.5 years; 128 endurance, 53 
      sprint) with normal preparticipation cardiac screening. Weighted least squares 
      allometric regression determined optimal scaling exponents for height, weight, 
      and BSA, with models advancing in complexity only when prediction accuracy 
      significantly improved. Equations were externally validated in 62 independent 
      elite male endurance cyclists. RESULTS: Men had larger BSA-scaled LV and left 
      atrial size than women (both P<0.001). Many cyclists exceeded conventional 
      thresholds (American Society of Echocardiography 2015: LV end-diastolic volume 
      indexed to BSA 69.6%, LV end-systolic volume indexed to BSA 62.9%; British 
      Society of Echocardiography 2020: LV end-diastolic volume indexed to BSA 50.8%, 
      left atrial volume indexed to BSA 28.7%). Height provided superior allometric fit 
      versus weight or BSA. Height, sex, discipline, and RR interval were key 
      determinants. On external validation, only 6.5% exceeded the Z score threshold 
      for LV end-diastolic volume indexed to BSA, compared with 90.3% classified 
      abnormal by ASE guidelines. CONCLUSIONS: Height-based allometric scaling 
      outperformed weight or BSA for cardiac dimensions and function in elite cyclists. 
      These sport-specific equations, validated in an independent cohort, provide 
      clinically usable percentage predicted values and Z scores that permit 
      quantitative assessment rather than binary thresholds and may reduce 
      misclassification of physiological adaptation as disease in preparticipation 
      cardiac screening programs.
