DP  - 2026 Oct 7
TI  - An 8-minute Self-Paced Cycling Test designed for endurance fitness monitoring: 
      Cross-sectional validity, test-retest reliability, and practical implementation.
PG  - 1-17
LID - 10.1080/02640414.2026.2741755 [doi]
AB  - Monitoring cycling fitness remains challenging. Most submaximal tests rely on 
      physiological responses to imposed workloads and overlook perceptual responses. 
      This study evaluated the cross-sectional convergent validity and test-retest 
      reliability of the Self-Paced Cycling Test (SCT-RPE), an 8-minute rating of 
      perceived effort (RPE)-regulated protocol. Twenty-one cyclists completed the 
      SCT-RPE, comprising three 2-minute stages at RPE Moderate, Strong, and Very 
      strong, followed by recovery and a 6-second sprint (P(peak)). Power 
      output-to-heart rate ratios (PO/HR) and weighted indices (wPO/HR) were calculated 
      at stage and overall levels. Validity was evaluated against graded exercise test 
      outcomes and sprint performance, and reliability across repeated trials. 
      wPO/HR(overall) was retained as a candidate endurance-fitness index because it 
      showed a strong association with peak work rate (W(peak), R(2) = 0.77, p < 0.001, 
      slope = 13.4 W per unit), acceptable reliability, and power-dominant weighting 
      aligned with the RPE-regulated design. Endurance-related variables showed 
      moderate-to-very-high interday reliability (intraclass correlation coefficients, 
      ICC(3,1) = 0.72-0.94; coefficients of variation of the typical error, 
      CV(TE) = 3.29%-7.97%). P(peak) showed high reliability (ICC(3,1) = 0.86; 
      CV(TE) = 8.96%) and an almost perfect correlation with sprint performance 
      (r = 0.93). The SCT-RPE showed cross-sectional convergent validity and acceptable 
      reliability; longitudinal responsiveness remains to be established.
