PMID- 42885854
OWN - NLM
STAT- Publisher
LR  - 20261016
IS  - 1530-0315 (Electronic)
IS  - 0195-9131 (Linking)
DP  - 2026 Oct 16
TI  - Consumer Grade Wearable Sensor Metrics Are Associated with Internal Forces at 
      Common Running Injury Locations.
LID - 10.1249/MSS.0000000000004191 [doi]
AB  - INTRODUCTION: Inexpensive consumer-grade wearable sensors have expanded 
      possibilities to assess, monitor, or modify a runner's gait. However, it is 
      unclear which sensor-measured gait metrics are strongly associated with internal 
      loading at common injury locations. PURPOSE: To measure the association between 
      gait metrics measured by consumer-grade wearable sensors and internal forces at 
      the Achilles tendon and the patellofemoral joint. METHODS: 55 healthy runners 
      wearing consumer-grade chest straps foot-pods completed a 38-minute run on an 
      instrumented treadmill at 12 speeds from 70%-125% of self-reported typical 
      running pace. A scaled musculoskeletal model derived from motion capture data and 
      driven by static optimization was used to calculate Achilles tendon and 
      patellofemoral joint forces during stance for each speed. Functional regression 
      models were used to estimate the associations of each of nine sensor-measured 
      gait metrics with internal forces across stance, after adjusting for body mass 
      and running speed. RESULTS: At both the Achilles tendon and the patellofemoral 
      joint, cadence, vertical oscillation, and vertical ratio (vertical oscillation 
      divided by step length) showed the strongest associations with internal forces 
      (maximum of 0.4-0.6 BW change in force per one standard deviation change in the 
      gait metric). Other wearable sensor-measured gait metrics, including stance time, 
      leg stiffness, and stance time percent showed weaker associations (<0.4 BW change 
      in force per one standard deviation change in the gait metric). CONCLUSIONS: 
      Runners with higher-than-average cadence, vertical oscillation, or vertical ratio 
      values experience marked differences in internal forces at the Achilles tendon 
      and patellofemoral joint. Of these gait metrics, cadence is the most practical 
      for out-of-lab use because of its ease of accurate measurement with 
      consumer-grade devices.
