PMID- 42772726
OWN - NLM
STAT- Publisher
LR  - 20260922
IS  - 1555-0273 (Electronic)
IS  - 1555-0265 (Linking)
DP  - 2026 Sep 22
TI  - Beyond the Metrics: A Case Study on Integrating Quantitative and Qualitative Data 
      to Monitor Training in Elite Rowers.
PG  - 1-11
LID - 10.1123/ijspp.2026-0183 [doi]
AB  - PURPOSE: Current athlete monitoring practices remain largely focused on training 
      load (TL), overlooking critical aspects of how training is executed. This study 
      explored how a mixed-methods approach integrating qualitative and quantitative 
      data could provide a more holistic evaluation of training quality in an elite 
      rowing environment. METHODS: A concurrent mixed-methods case study was conducted 
      with 19 elite female rowers (age 22-37; tier 4-5) preparing for the 2024 Paris 
      Olympics. Across 16 on-water sessions from 4 crews, traditional monitoring 
      data-duration, session rating of perceived exertion, and TL-were collected. 
      Athletes and coaches independently completed the Subjective Training Quality 
      scale following each session. Postsession coach-athlete debriefs were audio 
      recorded, transcribed, and subjected to manual deductive content and sentiment 
      analysis. Interrater reliability was assessed via intraclass correlation 
      coefficients. Qualitative data were quantized and integrated with quantitative 
      metrics through joint displays. RESULTS: Interrater reliability was excellent 
      (intraclass correlation coefficient = .992; 95% CI, .966-.998). Coaches 
      consistently rated Subjective Training Quality scores equal or higher than 
      athletes across all constructs, with discrepancies ranging from 0 to 3.5 points. 
      Technical aspects dominated debrief content, followed by mental and physical 
      themes. Sentiment varied considerably across sessions of comparable TL, 
      demonstrating that TL alone does not capture the full experiential nature of 
      training. CONCLUSIONS: Integrating Subjective Training Quality scores, 
      coach-athlete debrief analysis, and TL provides contextual insight into training 
      quality not accessible through conventional monitoring alone. This mixed-methods 
      framework offers a practical, minimally intrusive approach that captures nuanced 
      physical, technical, and mental dimensions of training, supporting more informed 
      coaching decisions and athlete development.
