TI  - Cardiorespiratory coupling tightens with workload during graded exercise: A pilot 
      study with adolescent athletes.
PG  - e70884
LID - 10.14814/phy2.70884 [doi]
LID - e70884
AB  - Cardiorespiratory coupling (CRC) reflects coordination between heart and lung 
      function, but how it changes with increasing intensity during graded exercise 
      remains unclear. We investigated CRC as real-time covariation between cardiac 
      timing and breath-by-breath pulmonary oxygen uptake (VO(2)) to test whether 
      coupling strengthens with workload in adolescent athletes. We conducted an 
      observational, within-subject analysis of the ACTES cycling dataset. Eighteen 
      adolescents cycled at 50, 110, and 140 W. Beat-to-beat RR intervals and pulmonary 
      VO(2) time series were examined in ultra-short 60 s segments. Fluctuations were 
      summarized using SDRR/RMSSD and SDVO(2)/RMSSDVO(2). CRC was measured using joint 
      symbolic dynamics (JSD; Shannon entropy, SE, and Miller-Madow-corrected entropy, 
      CSE) and the highest normalized cross-correlation (X-Corr). Mean RR decreased and 
      mean pulmonary VO(2) increased with workload (both p < 0.0001). SDRR and RMSSD 
      were lower at 110 and 140 W versus 50 W; SDVO(2) declined from 50 to 110/140 W, 
      whereas RMSSDVO(2) was unchanged. X-Corr increased from 50 to 110/140 W 
      (p ≤ 0.0014). JSD indices decreased as workload increased (SE: global p = 0.139; 
      CSE: global p = 0.029), suggesting tighter CRC. CRC becomes more pronounced with 
      increased workload, aligning with reduced heart rate variability and reflecting 
      vagal withdrawal and reflex responses that improve heart-lung integration in 
      adolescent athletes.
