TI  - Energy metabolism and muscle activation heterogeneity explain V̇O2 slow component 
      and muscle fatigue of cycling at different intensities.
PG  - 503-517
LID - 10.1113/EP090444 [doi]
AB  - NEW FINDINGS: What is the central question of this study? What are the 
      physiological mechanisms underlying muscle fatigue and the increase in the O(2) 
      cost per unit of work during high-intensity exercise? What is the main finding 
      and its importance? Muscle fatigue happens before, and does not explain, the V̇O2 
      slow component ( V̇O2sc ), but they share the same origin. Muscle activation 
      heterogeneity is associated with muscle fatigue and V̇O2sc . Knowing this may 
      improve training prescriptions for healthy people leading to improved public 
      health outcomes. ABSTRACT: This study aimed to explain the V̇O2 slow component ( 
      V̇O2sc ) and muscle fatigue during cycling at different intensities. The muscle 
      fatigue of 16 participants was determined through maximal isokinetic effort 
      lasting 3 s during constant work rate bouts of moderate (MOD), heavy (HVY) and 
      very heavy intensity (VHI) exercise. Breath-by-breath V̇O2 , near-infrared 
      spectroscopy signals and EMG activity were analysed (thigh muscles). V̇O2sc was 
      higher during VHI exercise (∼70% vs. ∼28% of V̇O2 reserve in HVY). The 
      deoxygenated haemoglobin final value during VHI exercise was higher than during 
      HVY and MOD exercise (∼90% of HHb physiological normalization, vs. ∼82% HVY and 
      ∼45% MOD). The muscle fatigue was greater after VHI exercise (∼22% vs. HVY ∼5%). 
      There was no muscle fatigue after MOD exercise. The greatest magnitude of muscle 
      fatigue occurred within 2 min (VHI ∼17%; HVY ∼9%), after which it stabilized. No 
      significant relationship between V̇O2sc and muscle force production was observed. 
      The τ of muscle V̇O2 was significantly related (R(2)  = 0.47) with torque 
      decrease for VHI. Type I and II muscle fibre recruitment mainly in the rectus 
      femoris moderately explained the muscle fatigue (R(2)  = 0.30 and 0.31, 
      respectively) and the V̇O2sc (R(2)  = 0.39 and 0.27, respectively). The V̇O2sc is 
      also partially explained by blood lactate accumulation (R(2)  = 0.42). In 
      conclusion muscle fatigue and O(2) cost seem to share the same physiological 
      cause linked with a decrease in the muscle V̇O2 and a change in lactate 
      accumulation. Muscle fatigue and V̇O2sc are associated with muscle activation 
      heterogeneity and metabolism of different muscles activated during cycling.
