DP  - 2026 Oct 22
TI  - Efficacy of blood flow restriction combined with low-intensity aerobic training 
      in patients with COPD: Study protocol for the BREATH randomized controlled trial.
PG  - 108515
LID - S1551-7144(26)00301-0 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.cct.2026.108515 [doi]
AB  - INTRODUCTION: Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) is characterized by 
      persistent airflow limitation, which reduces exercise capacity. Although current 
      guidelines recommend moderate-to-vigorous aerobic exercise to achieve optimal 
      physiological adaptations, many patients cannot sustain the required intensity, 
      limiting overall therapeutic benefit. This study protocol aims to evaluate the 
      effects of combining blood flow restriction (BFR) with aerobic training on 
      exercise capacity, cardiopulmonary function, and health-related quality of life 
      in patients with COPD, compared with conventional aerobic training or usual care. 
      METHODS: We will conduct a single-center, prospective, parallel-group randomized 
      controlled trial. 45 clinically stable patients with COPD (GOLD stages II-IV) 
      will be recruited. Eligible participants must have had no acute exacerbations in 
      the past two months and be able to participate in the exercise training program. 
      Participants will be randomly assigned to one of three groups: usual care, 
      conventional aerobic training (60-80% VO₂ peak), or BFR-enhanced aerobic training 
      (40-50% VO₂ peak, with 40-80% arterial occlusion pressure). All exercise sessions 
      will be supervised, three times per week for eight weeks. The primary outcome is 
      exercise capacity. Secondary outcomes include pulmonary function tests, 
      respiratory muscle strength, diaphragmatic ultrasound, heart rate variability, 
      and health-related quality-of-life questionnaires. PLANNED OUTCOMES: BFR-enhanced 
      aerobic training may be a safe and effective rehabilitation option for COPD 
      patients who cannot tolerate high-intensity exercise. The BREATH trial is 
      expected to provide essential evidence for clinical care by integrating BFR 
      training into pulmonary rehabilitation. This approach could inform future 
      management strategies for exercise intolerance among patients with COPD. TRIAL 
      REGISTRATION: The study has been approved by the Institutional Review Board 
      (approval number: B-ER-113-298) and registered on ClinicalTrials.gov 
      (registration number: NCT06756646).
