PMID- 42810917
OWN - NLM
STAT- Publisher
LR  - 20260929
IS  - 1878-1861 (Electronic)
IS  - 1878-1861 (Linking)
DP  - 2026 Sep 17
TI  - Running together vs. alone: Women's HRV adapts more in groups, men's more when 
      running solo.
LID - S1440-2440(26)00568-2 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.jsams.2026.09.011 [doi]
AB  - OBJECTIVES: Three objectives guided this study: (1) to test whether a brief, 
      two-week running intervention changes heart rate variability (HRV) and 
      mental-health parameters; (2) to examine whether running alone versus in groups 
      differentially affects these outcomes; and (3) to assess whether participants' 
      gender moderates these effects. DESIGN: 131 young adults with sedentary 
      lifestyles were randomized to an individual (solo) running condition, a 
      group-running condition, or a waitlist control, and were assessed before and 
      after the two-week intervention. METHODS: Primary self-report outcomes were 
      perceived stress, positive affect, negative affect, and depressive symptoms. 
      Autonomic regulation was indexed by different HRV measures quantifying both 
      parasympathetic and sympathetic aspects of autonomic function, derived from ECG 
      recordings obtained pre- and post-intervention. RESULTS: There were no general 
      effects of running on any HRV index, in either the solo or the group-running 
      condition. However, HRV changes were moderated by gender and running context, as 
      evidenced by significant TIME × GROUP × GENDER interactions in vagally mediated 
      (parasympathetic) indices: among women, HRV increased particularly in the 
      group-running condition, whereas among men, HRV increases were observed primarily 
      in the solo condition. CONCLUSIONS: This exploratory study indicates that HRV 
      might not generally adapt to physical activity, but rather depends on individual 
      or contextual factors such as gender and running context (in groups versus solo). 
      The findings reinforce the value of HRV metrics as a tool to further support the 
      role of physical activity in enhancing overall health and underscore the 
      importance of tailoring physical activity interventions to individual 
      characteristics.
