TI  - Relationship Between Objective Measures of Sleep and Nocturnal Cardiovascular 
      Physiology and Swimming Performance in Collegiate Female Athletes: A Bayesian 
      Analysis.
LID - 10.1519/JSC.0000000000005630 [doi]
AB  - Vatne, EA, Cencer, D, Vatne, M, Pulvermacher, K, Saenz, C, Kraemer, WJ, Robinson, 
      B, Jones, P, Quebedeaux, N, Sprague, R, Dial, M, Carpenter, T, and Hagen, J. 
      Relationship between objective measures of sleep and nocturnal cardiovascular 
      physiology and swimming performance in collegiate female athletes: A Bayesian 
      analysis. J Strength Cond Res XX(X): 000-000, 2026-There is substantial evidence 
      that sleep influences athletic performance; however, relationships with 
      real-world competition outcomes remain unclear. Therefore, the purpose of this 
      study was to explore the associations between objectively measured sleep, 
      nocturnal cardiovascular physiology on the night before competition, and swim 
      performance in female collegiate athletes. Female swimmers (n = 11) who wore a 
      sleep monitoring device the night before competition, competed in the 200-yard 
      freestyle (short course yard), and had ≥21 of 28 prior nights of valid data were 
      included. Sleep metrics included total sleep time (TST), time in bed (TiB), and 
      sleep efficiency (SE), whereas nocturnal cardiovascular metrics included heart 
      rate variability (HRV) and resting heart rate (RHR) standardized as 
      individualized z-scores. Race times were obtained from a publicly available 
      database. Bayesian linear mixed-effects models estimated posterior probabilities 
      of performance improvement. Results indicated a high probability that greater TST 
      and TiB were associated with faster race times, with substantial support for 
      improvements of at least 1 second per additional hour of sleep. In contrast, SE, 
      HRV, and RHR showed low posterior evidence of meaningful associations with 
      performance. These findings extend prior sleep and performance literature that 
      has been confined to proxy assessments of athletic performance in controlled 
      settings to real-world competition that should inform athlete education, 
      emphasizing the importance of sufficient sleep opportunity and duration before 
      competition.
