VI  - 8
DP  - 2026
TI  - Case Report: Metabolic adaptations to intensive aerobic training in Parkinson's 
      disease: autonomic and physiopathological response.
PG  - 1920699
LID - 10.3389/fspor.2026.1920699 [doi]
LID - 1920699
AB  - INTRODUCTION: Aerobic exercise is a key non-pharmacological intervention in 
      Parkinson's disease (PD), yet the physiological mechanisms underlying its 
      therapeutic effects remain incompletely understood. This case report aimed to 
      investigate metabolic and cardiovascular adaptations to an intensive aerobic 
      training programme in a person with PD, with particular focus on metabolic 
      efficiency and insulin-like growth factor 1 (IGF-1) response. METHODS: A 
      72-year-old man with idiopathic PD (Hoehn & Yahr stage II, ON medication) 
      participated in a 4-week intensive treadmill training programme (20 sessions) 
      within the METEX-PD protocol. Exercise was performed at a constant external 
      workload (5.0-5.5 km·h⁻(1); 6.0-8.8 METs). Heart rate (HR) was continuously 
      monitored, oxygen uptake ( V˙ O₂) was assessed using a portable metabolic 
      analyser, post-exercise blood lactate concentration was measured, and serum IGF-1 
      levels were evaluated. Assessments were conducted at baseline, mid-intervention, 
      and post-training. RESULTS: Progressive reductions in HR, V˙ O₂, measured METs, 
      and energy cost were observed throughout the intervention, indicating improved 
      aerobic efficiency despite the maintenance of a constant external workload. HR 
      responses across training sessions showed a peculiar pattern suggestive of 
      chronotropic incompetence and cardiovascular drift. Non metabolic 
      Hyperventilation occurred during post training testing. Serum IGF-1 levels 
      increased following the training period, whereas no acute exercise-induced 
      changes were detected. Blood lactate responses remained consistent with the 
      prescribed exercise intensity. DISCUSSION: Intensive aerobic training induced 
      favourable metabolic adaptations in this individual with PD, as evidenced by 
      improved aerobic efficiency and increased circulating IGF-1 levels. These 
      adaptations occurred despite potential autonomic dysfunction affecting 
      cardiovascular responses. The findings support the use of direct metabolic 
      measurements to better characterise training adaptations and optimise 
      individualised aerobic exercise prescription in people with PD. Further studies 
      are warranted to confirm these observations in larger cohorts.
