DP  - 2026 Sep
TI  - Position-specific differences in match performance of elite U-17 soccer players 
      using GPS-derived external load data: focus on forwards and defenders.
PG  - 48-53
LID - 10.20463/pan.2026.0039 [doi]
AB  - PURPOSE: The objective of this study was to investigate differences in match 
      performance characteristics between forwards and defenders among elite U-17 
      soccer players using Global Positioning System (GPS)-derived data. METHODS: This 
      study included 37 elite U-17 soccer players representing Gyeonggi Province. 
      External load data collected during official matches were analyzed. Variables 
      were classified as measures of aerobic capacity, anaerobic capacity, 
      neuromuscular performance, and agility and change-of-direction ability. 
      Between-group differences were assessed using independent samples t-tests. 
      RESULTS: No significant positional differences were observed in total distance 
      covered, distance per minute, mean speed, or low- to moderate-intensity activity 
      zones (p > 0.05). Compared with defenders, forwards demonstrated significantly 
      higher values for high-speed running distance, a higher proportion of 
      high-intensity activity, maximum speed, and high-intensity acceleration and 
      change-of-direction performance (p < 0.05). CONCLUSION: These findings suggest 
      that at the elite youth level, fundamental aerobic capacity is similarly 
      developed across playing positions, whereas high-intensity movement and 
      neuromuscular performance capacities vary according to playing roles and tactical 
      demands. Accordingly, training programs and player development strategies should 
      be designed to account for positional characteristics.
