TI  - Fatigability after 6 Weeks of Concurrent Dryland and Sprint Swimming Training.
LID - 10.1055/a-2965-7366 [doi]
AB  - This study examined whether the acute effects of dryland muscular endurance 
      training on subsequent strength and swimming responses differed before (T1) and 
      after (T2) 6 weeks of muscular endurance and sprint interval training. Sixteen 
      male and female swimmers (16.3±3.2 y) were ranked according to their 100-m 
      performance and divided into groups trained with muscular endurance and sprint 
      interval training (n=8) and sprint interval training (n=8). At T1 and T2, both 
      performed a 1-RM evaluation and completed muscular endurance and swimming and 
      swimming conditions in counterbalanced order. At T1 and T2 between conditions %Δ 
      of swimming performance, tethered force, hand grip, isometric shoulder strength, 
      blood lactate, stroke rate, stroke length, stroke index, and arm-stroke 
      efficiency were calculated: %Δ=([muscular 
      endurance+swimming-swimming]/swimming)x100. The 1-RM and external load were 
      increased in groups trained with muscular endurance and sprint interval training 
      only at T2. No differences (p>0.05) between groups were found in swimming 
      performance, tethered force, blood lactate, stroke rate, stroke length, stroke 
      index, and arm-stroke efficiency at T1 and T2. Isometric shoulder strength in 
      group trained with muscular endurance and sprint interval training decreased 
      after swimming at T1 and increased at T2 (-8.1±15.8 vs. 0.68±12.68%), whereas the 
      opposite occurred in group trained with sprint interval training (4.22±12.42 
      vs.-1.64±12.29%, interaction, p=0.02). Six weeks of concurrent muscular endurance 
      and sprint interval training improved strength and maintained swimming 
      performance despite increased external loading during the concurrent training 
      session.
