TI  - Phase-specific neuromechanical signatures of 40-m sprint performance: an 
      integrated two-dimensional kinematic and surface electromyographic profiling 
      study.
PG  - 1939386
LID - 10.3389/fphys.2026.1939386 [doi]
AB  - BACKGROUND: Sprint performance reflects coordinated mechanical and neuromuscular 
      behavior across distinct sprint phases. This study identified phase-specific 
      neuromechanical signatures of 40-m sprint performance and tested whether 
      integrated kinematic and electromyographic information added predictive value 
      beyond direct spatiotemporal determinants of running velocity. METHODS: In this 
      cross-sectional study, 160 young adults classified as sprint-trained athletes or 
      physically active non-sprinters completed three maximal 40-m sprints. Split times 
      were recorded every 10 m. Four synchronized high-speed cameras (240 frames·s(-1)) 
      and wireless surface electromyography of six lower-limb muscles captured 
      sagittal-plane kinematics, muscle excitation, coactivation, and 
      excitation-movement coupling across four sprint phases. Velocity-adjusted 
      mixed-effects models and one-dimensional statistical parametric mapping assessed 
      discrete and waveform differences. Reliability-qualified features were analyzed 
      using elastic-net models with repeated nested five-fold cross-validation. Mean 
      40-m velocity was the primary outcome. Step length and step frequency formed the 
      direct spatiotemporal benchmark; ground-contact and flight times were added in an 
      extended benchmark. Sprint-training-status classification was secondary and 
      exploratory. RESULTS: Sprint-trained athletes completed 40 m faster than 
      non-sprinters (5.20 ± 0.31 vs 5.95 ± 0.40 s). Their profile showed longer 
      normalized steps, shorter contact times, phase-specific trunk and lower-limb 
      configurations, greater early-phase gluteus maximus and vastus lateralis 
      excitation, and greater later-phase biceps femoris preactivation and medial 
      gastrocnemius excitation. In the complete synchronized sample ( n = 152 ), the 
      integrated non-spatiotemporal model achieved R2 = 0.74 and RMSE = 0.34 m·s(-1), 
      versus R2 = 0.60 /RMSE = 0.43 for kinematics and R2 = 0.55 /RMSE = 0.45 for sEMG. 
      Adding neuromechanical features to the direct benchmark improved R (2) from 0.72 
      to 0.86 and reduced RMSE from 0.36 to 0.25 m·s(-1) (paired reduction 0.11, 95% CI 
      0.07-0.15). Improvement persisted for the extended benchmark (R (2) 0.76→0.87; 
      RMSE 0.33→0.24). Transition and high-speed features accounted for 82% of 
      coefficient magnitude, not variance explained. Exploratory classification 
      achieved AUC = 0.94 and balanced accuracy = 0.89. CONCLUSION: Forty-metre sprint 
      performance was characterized by a phase-specific neuromechanical signature that 
      provided incremental out-of-fold predictive information beyond direct and closely 
      related spatiotemporal velocity metrics. External validation is required before 
      broader generalization.
