TI  - Tapering and Peaking in the Weight Lifting Sports: A Systematic Review of 
      Athletes' Self-Reported Strategies.
LID - 10.1007/s40279-026-02500-w [doi]
AB  - BACKGROUND: Tapering, the reduction of training load before competition, is 
      widely recognized as an important strategy for optimizing performance. While 
      tapering practices have been well documented in endurance sports, comparatively 
      less is known about their application in weight lifting sports such as CrossFit, 
      Highland Games, powerlifting, strongman and weightlifting. OBJECTIVES: To 
      systematically review the available evidence on athletes self-reported tapering 
      practices in weight lifting sports in accordance with PRISMA guidelines. METHODS: 
      An electronic search was performed using PubMed, Embase, SPORTDiscus and Scopus 
      for studies that examined athletes self-reported tapering practices in weight 
      lifting sports. Eligible studies included peer-reviewed journal articles only, 
      with no limit placed on date or language of publication. The methodological 
      quality of included studies was assessed using the AXIS critical appraisal tool 
      for cross-sectional studies. RESULTS: All seven studies were rated high quality 
      (≥ 80% score, low risk of bias); five used surveys and two powerlifting studies 
      used interviews. Tapering prevalence remained consistently high across sports, 
      with little variation by competition level, age, or bodyweight class. Step tapers 
      were most commonly reported, with linear and exponential models less frequent. 
      Taper length typically ranged from five to nine days, although longer durations 
      were reported in powerlifting. Weightlifting and CrossFit athletes most often 
      reported formal coaching, while self-coaching was more common in strongman and 
      Highland Games; female athletes generally reported greater coaching access than 
      males. Training cessation periods were between 1.5 and 4 days. Athletes typically 
      reached peak training volume 4-6 weeks and peak intensity 2-3 weeks before 
      competition, with taper phases characterized by a reduction in training volume 
      of ~ 40-45%. Deadlifts were typically last performed 6-8 days before competition, 
      squats 5-6.5 days, and the bench press 3-5 days. The most consistent taper 
      adjustment was reduced training duration, with CrossFit emphasizing aerobic and 
      gymnastics work, Highland Games and strongman prioritizing power and strength, 
      and weightlifting focusing on strength, power, and technical work. Additional 
      tapering strategies included nutrition and recovery methods. Athletes reported 
      tapering to enhance recovery, performance, and psychological readiness, while 
      ineffective tapering was linked to errors in load management and contextual 
      stressors such as work, travel, and nutrition. CONCLUSION: Tapering in weight 
      lifting sports is typically short and structured, with step tapers, reduced 
      training volume, and lift-specific timing most common, but effectiveness depends 
      on managing both training load and contextual stressors. These findings 
      underscore the multifactorial nature of tapering and highlight the need for 
      evidence based, sport specific recommendations.
