TI  - The role of age on neuromuscular performance decay induced by a maximal intensity 
      sprint session in a group of competitive endurance athletes.
LID - 10.4081/ejtm.2022.10378 [doi]
LID - 10378
AB  - Age-related changes in the neuromuscular system functions may affect profoundly 
      high-level athletes' performance across their careers. The present study aimed to 
      analyse the fatiguing effect of a maximal intensity sprint session (MISS) on 
      competitive athletes of different ages. Thirty-one competitive endurance athletes 
      completed a knee extensors and flexors' maximal-voluntary-isometric-contraction 
      (MVC) test before and after a maximal-intensity-sprint-session (MISS) consisting 
      of 4x15s Wingate-tests. The data have been stratified considering three age 
      categories (18-28, n=11, 29-38; n=10; 39-43, n=10). Overall, both quadricep and 
      hamstring muscles early and late rate of torque development (RTD) dropped 
      significantly more than the maximal voluntary torque (MVT) (p<.05). Age had a 
      significant effect on early RTD, with older athletes exhibiting greater RTD 
      (p<.05). A significant effect of age also emerged for the changes in surface sEMG 
      variables, in which the frequency spectrum variables dropped significantly more 
      than the sEMG amplitude (RMS) (p<.05). The dynamics of changes in neuromuscular 
      performance markers after a MISS suggested that getting older competitive 
      athletes may potentially experience a greater loss in early explosive strength 
      compared to maximal or late explosive strength.
