DP  - 2027 Winter
TI  - Changes in Hormone Cycles and Exercise Capacity During Testosterone Therapy in a 
      Transmasculine Triathlete: A Case Study.
PG  - e00073
LID - 10.1249/ESM.0000000000000073 [doi]
AB  - INTRODUCTION: The impact of testosterone therapy on the hormone cycles, exercise 
      capacity, and physiology of transmasculine individuals is not well understood. 
      Existing studies report limited metrics at large time intervals between data 
      collection points. METHODS: High-resolution temporal data were collected from a 
      single amateur trans male triathlete over 13 months-including one baseline month 
      and 12 months on testosterone therapy-to characterize hormone, strength, body 
      composition, aerobic, and training load profiles. RESULTS: Daily urine hormone 
      monitoring revealed that progesterone and luteinizing hormone are the clearest 
      metrics associated with cessation of menses. After 1 yr, the participant 
      increased lean body mass by 12%, average hand grip strength by 16%, jump height 
      by 16%, and average knee isometric strength by 18%, but did not lose fat mass or 
      show changes in isokinetic knee strength. Fluctuations in running fitness and 
      maximum oxygen consumption ( V˙O2max ) were associated with both training and 
      testosterone levels, with future work needed to untangle their relative 
      contributions. CONCLUSION: This study provides the most comprehensive 
      characterization of the impacts of testosterone therapy on hormone cycles, 
      exercise capacity, and physiological changes in a single person to date, as well 
      as observations to help inform the design of larger cohort studies.
