TI  - Concurrent Changes in Body Composition, Body Image, and Eating Behaviors in NCAA 
      Division 1 Female Distance Runners Across an Indoor and Outdoor Track and Field 
      Season.
LID - 10.1519/JSC.0000000000005535 [doi]
AB  - Parnell, S, Kaylor, MG, Johnson, MF, Addison, A, Braun-Trocchio, R, and Graybeal, 
      AJ. Concurrent changes in body composition, body image, and eating behaviors in 
      NCAA Division 1 female distance runners across an indoor and outdoor track and 
      field season. J Strength Cond Res XX(X): 000-000, 2026-This exploratory study 
      evaluated concurrent body composition and psychological changes across 
      competitive indoor and outdoor track seasons in NCAA Division I (DI) female 
      distance runners. Eleven athletes (body mass index: 20.2 ± 1.3 kg·m-2; body fat 
      percentage: 21.7 ± 3.1%; Cross-country 5k-PR: 18.8 ± 1.2 minutes; Miles/wk: 43 ± 
      9) completed anthropometric, body composition, body image (BI), disordered eating 
      behaviors (DE), mood, depression, and female athlete triad risk factor (TRF) 
      assessments on 3 occasions throughout a competitive track season (preseason, 
      end-of-indoor, end-of-outdoor). Training loads and social media usage were also 
      recorded. Significance was set at p < 0.050. Total, appendicular, and leg lean 
      soft tissue (LST) increased from preseason-to-indoor (p ≤ 0.043), whereas weight 
      and total, appendicular, and leg LST decreased from indoor-to-outdoor (p ≤ 
      0.038). Fat mass remained stable throughout. BI distortion and dissatisfaction 
      were present at all timepoints. Although group-level psychological outcomes did 
      not significantly change, early-season increases in body size were significantly 
      associated with worsening BI, DE, and depression, with these adverse patterns 
      persisting despite subsequent, later-season reductions in body size. Greater 
      social media usage was significantly associated with weight loss and depression, 
      and higher training loads were associated with DE severity. All athletes 
      demonstrated low energy availability (EA) and accumulated additional TRF 
      throughout. In this cohort of DI female distance runners, subtle seasonal 
      fluctuations in body composition were associated with maladaptive BI and DE that 
      persists despite weight loss. These findings highlight the importance of 
      proactive, season-long monitoring by coaches and strength and conditioning 
      professionals and programming strategies focused on preserving lean tissue, 
      supporting EA, and mitigating sociocultural pressures endemic to endurance sport 
      culture.
