PMID- 42812957
OWN - NLM
STAT- PubMed-not-MEDLINE
DCOM- 20260930
LR  - 20261001
IS  - 2035-8164 (Electronic)
IS  - 2035-8237 (Print)
IS  - 2035-8164 (Linking)
VI  - 18
DP  - 2026
TI  - Cardiac Adaptations in Endurance Athletes: A Bibliometric Analysis of 
      Exercise‑Induced Physiologic Remodeling.
PG  - 171224
LID - 10.52965/001c.171224 [doi]
LID - 171224
AB  - BACKGROUND: Endurance exercise induces cardiovascular adaptations known as 
      exercise-induced physiologic remodeling of the athlete's heart, including sinus 
      bradycardia, chamber enlargement, enhanced vagal tone, and atrial and ventricular 
      remodeling. These changes are generally physiologic, but concern remains about 
      their association with arrhythmias and long-term outcomes. This study aimed to 
      characterize the research landscape, identify influential contributors, and 
      explore emerging themes in this field. METHODS: A bibliometric analysis was 
      conducted using the Web of Science Core Collection database. Publications were 
      retrieved from inception through June 11, 2026, yielding 754 results. Annual 
      trends, leading countries, institutions, collaboration networks, and keyword 
      co-occurrence were analyzed using VOSviewer and organized using Microsoft Excel. 
      RESULTS: Publication output grew steadily from the 1990s and plateaued from the 
      mid-2010s onward, without a sharp inflection point. The United States (146) and 
      Italy (126) led national output, followed by England (87) and Australia (77); 36 
      countries met the network threshold. Institutional output was distributed, led by 
      Liverpool John Moores University, Harvard University, and KU Leuven, while 
      citation strength clustered around Belgian and Italian hubs. Exercise, 
      echocardiography, athlete's heart, and endurance emerged as dominant keywords. 
      Top-cited works date largely to 1994-2002, with citations accumulating over 
      decades. CONCLUSIONS: This field has grown through incremental refinement of a 
      stable question rather than a single catalyst, with a distributed evidence base. 
      Influential work treats athlete's hearts as benign by default, while emerging 
      themes suggest growing attention toward identifying which athletes deviate toward 
      pathologic remodeling. Cross-hub collaboration is needed to resolve whether an 
      athlete's heart is one phenomenon or several.
